      "id": 1,
      "text": "Johnstown coalesced as a small rural community in the early twentieth century during a surge of homesteading in Wyoming.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/johnstown_wyoming"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Johnstown lies within the Wind River Basin and is bordered by the Wind River Indian Reservation to the north.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        16
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/johnstown_wyoming"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The Johnstown Canal was constructed in 1898 using Shoshone labor to divert water from the Wind River for agricultural use.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        28
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/johnstown_wyoming"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Early residents of Johnstown focused on dryland farming, livestock, and supporting regional coal mining operations.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        26
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/johnstown_wyoming"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Wetlands and floodplains along local streams offer critical ecological corridors within the Johnstown area.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        6
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/johnstown_wyoming"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "The community of Johnstown features a suburban-rural mix with a high rate of single-family homeownership.",
